if it seems like the US is constantly  about to hit the debt limit also known  as the debt ceiling it's because it is  it's kind of like the horror film that  Hollywood keeps making sequels of even  though no one liked the first one every  few years or sometimes months Congress  needs to vote to raise it and they will  need to do it again sometime in 2023  when Congress will look like this a  republican held house and a democratic  held Senate with a democrat in the White  House and Republicans plan on using the  debt ceiling as leverage to negotiate  spending cuts the debt ceiling needs to  be lifted but wouldn't it be a normal  thing if we're 31 trillion dollars in  debt not to just give a blank check so  while Congress still looks like this  Democrats are looking to act on the debt  ceiling so it's not something  Republicans can use next year here are  three things they might do in that lame  duck session  but first a brief history lesson from  what happened another time Congress  looked like this the debt limit was  raised many times through recent history  without much debate until 2011 when  Republicans decided to use it as a  bargaining chip to negotiate for more  spending cuts the American people will  not accept an increase in the debt limit  without significant spending cuts and  reforms the three sides debated as the  U.S got closer and closer to defaulting  until Congressional leaders reached a  deal with the president just one day  before Uncle Sam hit his credit limit  the U.S didn't default but the country's  perfect AAA rating was downgraded by s p  in part because of the fight where no  longer the gold standard as it relates  to the world's economy longer term we  may see a rise in borrowing costs if  history repeats itself that is a scary  scenario for this government the U.S  can't default on its debt that would  have huge implications for the economy  worldwide even just getting close to the  debt limit can cause big problems for  the economy So to avoid a fight next  year Democrats this year have three  options  first is to just get rid of the debt  limit altogether the US is one of two  countries that even have one in the  first place and there's already  legislation introduced to make it like  all these other countries so the  treasury could just take on as much debt  as it needs to pay for spending that  Congress already voted for  treasury secretary Janet Yellen was  asked last year if she'd support the  repeal yes I would I believe it's very  destructive to put the president and  myself the treasury secretary in the  situation where we might be unable to  pay the bills that result from those  past decisions but Biden says he  wouldn't sign it I mean just say we  don't have a debt limit  no so that's the least likely  possibility the second is to just raise  the debt limit so high it's not really a  problem that's what the other country  with the debt limit Denmark does its  debt would have to quadruple to reach  its current limit the likely plan  wouldn't be to raise the US's that high  but just enough to remove it as  something that would need to be debated  in 2023 it's not due till next year but  the Democrats could pass something  potentially with Republican votes also  in the Senate that would raise it so we  didn't have to worry about it say till  2024 2025.  but the most likely plan is to  restructure the way the debt ceiling is  raised in the first place and make it  not congress's problem the idea  originates from that 2011 battle it was  an idea that was brought forward by now  Senate minority leader Mitch McConnell  who at the time was looking for ways to  help his members avoid having to vote  for the debt ceiling but give them way  to vote against it currently a majority  in the house and the Senate have to vote  for raising the debt ceiling and then it  signed or vetoed by the president like  most bills but members of Congress  especially Republicans don't like having  to vote for raising the debt ceiling so  what several bipartisan plans would do  is essentially reverse this it would  give the executive branch so the  president or even the treasury secretary  the authority to raise the debt limit  and Congress could pass a joint  resolution of disapproval basically  Congress instead of voting to  affirmatively raise the debt ceiling  members of Congress could vote basically  against raising the debt ceiling but to  act actually override the executive  branch they would need a two-thirds  majority vote  however not everyone wants to take the  debate away from Congress simply because  it's crisises that make Congress  actually get things done we find  ourselves at the edge of cliffs a lot  and Congress and that seems to be some  of the one of the only motivators to get  people to make concessions and and reach  agreements but on the debt ceiling that  is a very high risk strategy many  Republicans want that high-risk strategy  to get spending cuts in other measures  they worry they won't get under a  Democratic president Republicans have  tried to work with reach across the  aisle and have been shut out of many of  those conversations if we could do that  then we wouldn't need to uh you know  threaten you know use the threat of the  debt ceiling as a negotiating tool there  are definitely divisions within the  Republican Party about how they see that  some of them really want it want to go  to the brink and you know Force these  changes that they really believe are  important and others don't think we  should be using the debt ceiling that  way and would rather just take care of  it maybe even would rather Democrats  take care of it in the lame duck before  they have to worry about it next year in  their majority so Democrats have about  two months to decide on one of these  paths and potentially gain Republican  support for it  otherwise come 2023 we'll likely see an  epic debt limit debate again
 

